.\design\activity\project.json:387:          "content": "I’ll inspect the repository’s setup instructions, package manifests, and current dependency state, then perform only the setup steps the project explicitly defines and verify the development checks that are available.\n\nThe repository defines three dependency sets: root tooling plus `app` and `desktop`. The documented `install:all` installs only the two subprojects, so I’m also checking the root tooling explicitly; telemetry `.env` files are optional and I will not create empty local copies.\n\nNode.js 22.16.0 is installed, but VS Code’s auto-attach debugger is injected into terminal Node processes and caused the first npm probe to stall. I’ll clear that injection only for setup commands, then install the locked dependencies in each project.\n\nAll three dependency sets installed successfully. I’m now validating the prepared workspace with each subproject’s required lint and test commands; dependency deprecation notices came only from transitive Electron-side packages.\n\nLint and test validation is still running across both subprojects. No failures have been returned yet; the app test suite is the longer-running part.\n\nThe combined validation wrapper hit its timeout while the app’s Vitest process was still running, so it did not preserve the completed check outputs. I’m rerunning the checks independently with an appropriate test timeout so the final result is unambiguous.\n\nBoth linters pass cleanly. The desktop test runner is active but has not emitted per-file progress; I’m allowing the project’s full suite time to complete rather than treating the earlier wrapper timeout as a test failure.\n\nThe app suite passes all 1,223 tests. Desktop completed 447/451, with four failures concentrated in Git/filesystem tests: three hit the fixed 5-second timeout and one timing-sensitive worktree assertion then left a locked temp directory. I’m rerunning only those files with one worker to distinguish environmental contention from a real setup problem.\n\nThe isolated desktop rerun is still executing, which confirms this machine’s Git-backed tests are unusually slow relative to their 5-second per-test limits. I’m waiting for the final pass/fail output before deciding whether any project change is warranted.\n\nThe four previously failing desktop files pass when serialized. I’m now running the entire desktop suite with the same one-worker setting so the final development baseline reflects a complete passing suite, not just targeted reruns.",
